@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "FOREIGN KEY";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            FOREIGN KEY
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            A FOREIGN KEY is a SQL Server constraint used to create
            a relationship between two tables. It ensures that a
            value in one table refers to a valid record in another
            table.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS FOREIGN KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a FOREIGN KEY?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A FOREIGN KEY is a column or combination of columns that
            refers to the PRIMARY KEY or a UNIQUE key of another table.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is mainly used to maintain relationships and referential
            integrity between tables.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Simple Example
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                A Courses table can have CourseId as its PRIMARY KEY.
                The Students table can use CourseId as a FOREIGN KEY
                to connect students with courses.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- WHY FOREIGN KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Why Do We Need a FOREIGN KEY?
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Creates a relationship between two tables.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Maintains referential integrity.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Prevents invalid references between related tables.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Helps organize related data.
            </li>

            <li>
                Represents relationships in a relational database.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- PARENT CHILD -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Parent Table and Child Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            A FOREIGN KEY relationship normally consists of a parent
            table and a child table.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Table</th>
                        <th>Key</th>
                        <th>Role</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Courses</td>
                        <td>CourseId</td>
                        <td>PRIMARY KEY</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Students</td>
                        <td>CourseId</td>
                        <td>FOREIGN KEY</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- CREATE PARENT TABLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Step 1: Create Parent Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            First, create the table that contains the referenced key.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Courses
(
    CourseId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    CourseName VARCHAR(100)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- CREATE CHILD TABLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Step 2: Create Child Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            The Students table can then reference CourseId from the
            Courses table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    CourseId INT,

    CONSTRAINT FK_Students_Courses
    FOREIGN KEY (CourseId)
    REFERENCES Courses(CourseId)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- RELATIONSHIP DIAGRAM -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            FOREIGN KEY Relationship
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Server
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>
                                                    Courses
                                                       |
                                                       | CourseId
                                                       | PRIMARY KEY
                                                       ↓
                                                    Students
                                                       |
                                                       | CourseId
                                                       | FOREIGN KEY
                                                       ↓
                                           References Courses.CourseId</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT DATA -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert Valid Data
        </h2>

        <p>
            A foreign key value should refer to an existing value in
            the parent table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Courses
VALUES (1, 'Java');

INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (101, 'Rahul', 1);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-success border-0">

            <strong>Result:</strong>
            The insert is successful because CourseId 1 exists in
            the Courses table.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INVALID DATA -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Invalid FOREIGN KEY Value
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose CourseId 10 does not exist in the Courses table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (102, 'Priya', 10);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-danger border-0">

            <strong>Error:</strong>
            The insert fails because CourseId 10 does not exist in
            the parent Courses table.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ADD FOREIGN KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Add FOREIGN KEY to Existing Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            A FOREIGN KEY can also be added after creating the table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>ALTER TABLE Students
ADD CONSTRAINT FK_Students_Courses
FOREIGN KEY (CourseId)
REFERENCES Courses(CourseId);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- CASCADE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ON DELETE CASCADE
        </h2>

        <p>
            ON DELETE CASCADE automatically removes related child
            records when the corresponding parent record is deleted.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    CourseId INT,

    CONSTRAINT FK_Students_Courses
    FOREIGN KEY (CourseId)
    REFERENCES Courses(CourseId)
    ON DELETE CASCADE
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- FOREIGN KEY OUTPUT -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Output
        </h2>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Courses Table
        </h5>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>CourseId</th>
                        <th>CourseName</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>1</td>
                        <td>Java</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>2</td>
                        <td>.NET</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Students Table
        </h5>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>CourseId</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>1</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>2</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h5>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    FOREIGN KEY creates a relationship between tables.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    It normally references a PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE key.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Invalid references are prevented.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A table can have multiple FOREIGN KEY constraints.
                </li>

                <li>
                    ON DELETE CASCADE can be used when appropriate.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->
    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how FOREIGN KEY creates a
                relationship between tables, how to create and add a
                FOREIGN KEY, and how referential integrity prevents
                invalid references.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PREVIOUS / NEXT -->

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center
                mt-5 pt-4 border-top">

        <!-- PREVIOUS -->

        <a href="/SQLServer/Constraints/PrimaryKey"
           class="btn btn-outline-primary">

            ← Previous: PRIMARY KEY

        </a>


